SELECT 'core-2026-09-v4','possessive-s','87b630c771d79eac83e3be02f06e226cf9455e201ca4d4e9ebd574542396373d',payload || '{"slug":"possessive-s","title":"Possessive ’s","level":"A1","order":7,"summary":"Show ownership with singular nouns, regular plurals and irregular plurals.","learningGoals":["One owner","Plural owners","Read the whole phrase"],"guidance":"Read the rules and examples, then try a 20-question session. Explain why your chosen form fits the context and revisit any missed skill.","rules":[{"title":"One owner","explanation":"Add apostrophe plus s to a singular owner noun in the forms practised here. The owner comes before the thing owned.","examples":["the explorer’s compass","the rabbit’s hutch"]},{"title":"Plural owners","explanation":"For a regular plural already ending in s, add only an apostrophe. For an irregular plural without final s, add apostrophe plus s.","examples":["the explorers’ compasses","the children’s drawings"]},{"title":"Read the whole phrase","explanation":"The owner phrase determines apostrophe placement. The owned object can itself be singular or plural. Names ending in s have additional conventions outside this bank.","examples":["one student’s notebooks","several students’ classroom"]}],"tips":["Check both the meaning and the form before answering.","Use the explanation to understand a mistake before moving on.","A practice result is feedback, not an official language certificate."],"source":"seed","provenance":{"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","authorship":"Grammacho; generated offline with AI assistance","kind":"original-guidance-aligned","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"assessment":"Practice only; approximate teaching labels, not validated CEFR assessment."},"chapter":{"introduction":["Possessive marking connects an owner or related participant with a noun. It can express literal ownership, family relationships, authorship, time and other associations. First identify the owner and whether its noun is singular or plural; then place the apostrophe accordingly."],"rules":[{"title":"One owner","explanation":"Add apostrophe plus s to a singular owner noun in the forms practised here. The owner comes before the thing owned.","examples":["the explorer’s compass","the rabbit’s hutch","the **student’s** answer = one student"],"pattern":"one teacher → the teacher**’s** desk","paragraphs":["A singular owner normally takes apostrophe + s. The owned noun follows the marked owner: the teacher’s desk. The number of possessions does not determine the apostrophe: the teacher’s books still has one teacher. Names ending in s have style-dependent conventions, so follow the stated style."],"id":"possessive-s-foundation-1","domain":"noun-phrases","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"readingVersion":"foundations-2026-09-v2"},{"title":"Plural owners","explanation":"For a regular plural already ending in s, add only an apostrophe. For an irregular plural without final s, add apostrophe plus s.","examples":["the explorers’ compasses","the children’s drawings","the **students’** answers = several students"],"pattern":"several teachers → the teachers**’** desks","paragraphs":["A regular plural ending in s takes an apostrophe after that s. An irregular plural without final s takes ’s: children’s, women’s, people’s. The contrast is about the owner’s form, not whether the thing owned is singular or plural."],"id":"possessive-s-foundation-2","domain":"noun-phrases","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"readingVersion":"foundations-2026-09-v2"},{"title":"Read the whole phrase","explanation":"The owner phrase determines apostrophe placement. The owned object can itself be singular or plural. Names ending in s have additional conventions outside this bank.","examples":["one student’s notebooks","several students’ classroom","the **children’s** playground = several children"],"pattern":"children → the children**’s** books","paragraphs":["The possessive phrase normally places the owner before the owned noun. An of phrase is another way of expressing many relationships, especially with longer or inanimate noun phrases. It is not always stylistically interchangeable, so learn common patterns."],"id":"possessive-s-foundation-3","domain":"noun-phrases","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"readingVersion":"foundations-2026-09-v2"},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Its, hers, yours, ours and theirs are possessive forms without apostrophes. It’s is a contraction. In coordinated possession, Sam and Jo’s house often suggests joint possession; Sam’s and Jo’s houses suggests separate relationships. The apostrophe attaches to the owner phrase.","examples":["the **student’s** answer = one student","the **students’** answers = several students","the **children’s** playground = several children"],"id":"possessive-s-foundation-4","domain":"noun-phrases","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"readingVersion":"foundations-2026-09-v2"},{"domain":"noun-phrases","id":"genitive-alternatives","title":"Possessive forms, of-phrases and double genitives","explanation":"The genitive can express possession, origin, relationship or time. ’s often works well with people, groups and time expressions; an of-phrase is useful for many inanimate relationships and longer modifiers. An independent possessive after of can select one from a set: a friend of mine. The choice also affects focus and rhythm.","pattern":"Maya**’s** idea / the end **of** the road","examples":["It is **a neighbour of ours**.","We need **a day’s rest**."],"commonMistakes":["A photo of Maya depicts her; a photo of Maya’s belongs to or is associated with her. The two expressions need not describe the same relationship."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},{"domain":"noun-phrases","id":"joint-and-independent-possession","title":"Joint owners, separate owners and omitted nouns","explanation":"A shared possession commonly has one genitive ending after the coordinated owners. Separate possessions can mark each owner separately. A possessive can stand alone when its noun is recoverable, especially for homes and businesses. With a long possessor phrase, ’s attaches at its right edge in ordinary phrasal genitives.","pattern":"**Ana and Bo’s** house / **Ana’s and Bo’s** houses","examples":["We met **at my uncle’s**.","That is **the woman next door’s** bicycle."],"commonMistakes":["Long phrasal genitives can be hard to read; rephrase when the relationship becomes unclear. Plural possessive spelling still depends on the plural noun’s ending."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"}],"recap":["one teacher → the teacher**’s** desk","several teachers → the teachers**’** desks","children → the children**’s** books"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["articles"],"readingReferenceIds":["bc-possessive-s","cambridge-comprehensive-scope"]}'::jsonb FROM grammar.content_catalog_topics WHERE version='core-2026-09-v3' AND slug='possessive-s'
